627","time_limit":40},{"id":"la_batch9_determinant_011","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why is it misleading to treat $|A|$ exactly like the absolute value of a number?","options":["For a matrix, $|A|$ is the absolute value of the determinant.","For a matrix, $|A|$ is the determinant, which may be negative.","For a matrix, $|A|$ takes the absolute value of every entry.","For a matrix, $|A|$ is the length of $A$ as a vector of entries."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"6726d4f6cefaf65fa78dfe20","time_limit":35},{"id":"la_batch9_determinant_014","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"If $\\det(A)=-2$ and $\\det(B)=4$, what is $\\det(AB)$?","options":["$-6$","$2$","$16$","$-8$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"70114c11dcbdd6494c031dab","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch9_determinant_019","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"If $A$ and $B$ are square matrices and $\\det(A)=0$, which conclusion follows about $AB$?","options":["$AB$ is singular.","$AB$ must be the zero matrix.","$AB$ must equal $BA$.","$AB$ must have determinant $1$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"6988908ab20fd0052256ba6e","time_limit":40},{"id":"la_batch9_determinant_017_curated20261027_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"A $2\\times2$ matrix $\\boldsymbol{A}$ has $\\det(\\boldsymbol{A})=-3$. What happens to the unit square under $\\boldsymbol{x}\\mapsto\\boldsymbol{A}\\boldsymbol{x}$?","options":["It becomes a parallelogram of area $3$ with reversed orientation.","It becomes a parallelogram of area $-3$.","It becomes a parallelogram of area $3$ with the same orientation.","It collapses to zero area, since a negative determinant makes $\\boldsymbol{A}$ singular."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d05aaecd8a62b6a6dda848d4","time_limit":35},{"id":"la_batch9_determinant_018_curated20261027_002","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which identity holds for all $2\\times2$ matrices $\\boldsymbol{A}$ and $\\boldsymbol{B}$?","options":["$\\det(\\boldsymbol{A}\\boldsymbol{B})=\\det(\\boldsymbol{B}\\boldsymbol{A})$","$\\det(\\boldsymbol{A}+\\boldsymbol{B})=\\det(\\boldsymbol{A})+\\det(\\boldsymbol{B})$","$\\det(-\\boldsymbol{A})=-\\det(\\boldsymbol{A})$","$\\det(\\boldsymbol{A}\\boldsymbol{B})=\\det(\\boldsymbol{A})+\\det(\\boldsymbol{B})$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"8848665e84075abeec51140b","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_1b_math_ii_linear_algebra_determinant_2a40c735_topup20261028_001","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Let $\\boldsymbol{A}=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0&0\\\\0&1&0\\end{pmatrix}$, a $2\\times3$ matrix, and $\\boldsymbol{B}=\\boldsymbol{A}^{\\top}$, a $3\\times2$ matrix, so that $\\boldsymbol{A}\\boldsymbol{B}$ is $2\\times2$ and $\\boldsymbol{B}\\boldsymbol{A}$ is $3\\times3$. Which statement about their determinants is correct?","options":["$\\det(\\boldsymbol{A}\\boldsymbol{B})=\\det(\\boldsymbol{B}\\boldsymbol{A})=1$, by the product rule","Neither $\\boldsymbol{A}\\boldsymbol{B}$ nor $\\boldsymbol{B}\\boldsymbol{A}$ has a determinant","$\\det(\\boldsymbol{A}\\boldsymbol{B})=1$ and $\\det(\\boldsymbol{B}\\boldsymbol{A})=0$","$\\det(\\boldsymbol{A}\\boldsymbol{B})=\\det(\\boldsymbol{B}\\boldsymbol{A})=0$, since $\\det(\\boldsymbol{A})=0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f8b4ea34127796a0ea340d92","time_limit":40}]}}